# Hostile audit — two-phase bad-corridor boundary charge

**Date:** 2026-09-13  
**Reviewed:** `target_c_two_phase_bad_corridor_boundary_charge_2026-09-13.md`  
**Disposition:** PASS as a scoped proof-only candidate; C-II remains open.  
**Theorem authority:** unchanged; canonical Target C remains through Section 123.

## 1. Shared-boundary estimate

The proposed estimate is

\[
 \sum_i H_{D,i}\le(1+o(1))\sum_i I_{D,i},
\]

for convex exceptional faces contained in a convex force hull converging in scaled
Hausdorff distance to a disk, with each exceptional face having `o(D^2)` area.
The side lengths are one in the application, so length and occurrence count agree.

For one shared boundary chain `Gamma`, the complementary face path has length at
least the chord joining the chain endpoints.  A shared chain of length comparable
to `D` would contain a fixed positive angular arc of the limiting circle (or a
fixed nondegenerate sector if it winds around), and convexity would force a positive
scaled area in the exceptional face.  Thus all shared chains have `o(D)` length.

On a convex body trapped between concentric radii `D/2+o(D)`, a boundary chain of
length `o(D)` has total turning `o(1)` and chord length
`(1-o(1))` times its length.  Applying the complementary-path chord bound to the
alternating shared/free chains gives the stated aggregate estimate.  The estimate
must be read as an asymptotic convex-boundary lemma; it is not a claim for arbitrary
near-disk sets without convexity.

**Verdict:** PASS at the stated geometric scope.  The turning/chord estimate is the
load-bearing sublemma and should remain explicitly cited if this result is reused.

## 2. Strict-face dependency

The occurrence count `S_i<=4` requires that a face not contain a collinear chain
subdivided into many unit edges. Canonical Section 82 supplies this in the contact
core: a bounded face is a simple cycle; an unloaded angle equal to `pi` contradicts
positive equilibrium together with the unloaded degree lower bound three, and a
loaded angle equal to `pi` is excluded by the strict tangent cone of the loaded force
hull. Hence the bounded faces used here are strict convex polygons. This dependency
must remain explicit; the statement would be false for an arbitrary weakly convex
polygon with collinear unit subdivisions.

**Verdict:** PASS under the canonical strict-face dependency.

## 3. Two-phase incidence count

A convex face has at most two sides in each selected projective direction, hence at
most four selected-direction side occurrences.  With `S_i` the non-hull selected
occurrences and `U_i` the non-hull off-phase occurrences,

\[
 I_i=S_i+U_i,\qquad S_i\le4.
\]

For a bad face (`U_i\ge1`), the basic bound gives `I_i\le U_i+4\le5U_i`. Combining with the shared-
boundary estimate yields

\[
 \sum_{i\ \mathrm{bad}}H_i\le5(1+o(1))\sum_iU_i+o(D),
\]

where the `o(D)` good-face term is justified as follows.  A good face has a non-hull
selected phase path of at most four edges; otherwise its whole simple boundary would
be the force-hull boundary, incompatible with a subquadratic exceptional face.  The
possible signed phase words form a finite set.  For each nonzero displacement, a
convex polygon has at most two ordered force-hull vertex pairs with that displacement,
so only `O(1)` good-face endpoint realizations exist.  Each such realization has
`o(D)` shared boundary by the same near-disk/subquadratic-area argument.

**Verdict:** PASS.  This is an occurrence-safe physical-side count; it does not
replace `U` by `Q`.

The factor five is sharp at local incidence level. The strict unit hexagon with
cyclic vectors `u,v,w,-u,-v,-w` at `theta=70` degrees has four selected sides and
two off-phase sides. Marking one off-phase side as hull and one as non-hull gives
`H=1,S=4,U=1,I=5` with exact vector closure. This does not realize a global C-II
face, but it blocks any factor-four improvement that ignores the hull-side occurrence.

## 4. Equality-branch composition

Under Type-II `c_B=0`, every loaded-loaded unit contact is a force-hull side.  Under
global two-phase purity, at most four such sides are phase-aligned.  Every other
non-bridge loaded-hull side borders a bounded exceptional face; outer-visible bridges
are charged separately.  Therefore, in the scoped branch

\[
 k=o(D),\qquad u_B=o(D),\qquad B_o=o(D),
\]

the exact identity `e_BB=Q+2k+u_B` gives

\[
 \sum_iH_i\ge Q-o(D).
\]

The result consequently forces

\[
 U\ge Q/5-o(D).
\]

Using `H+I=P_exc=4f+Q` together with `H\ge Q-o(D)` and
`H\le(1+o(1))I` also gives `Q\le4f+o(D)`. This is a face-count consequence,
not an upper bound on `f` or `U`.

This implication does not assert that `U=o(D)`, and it does not turn a linear
exceptional-face defect into a contradiction.

**Verdict:** PASS at scoped proof-only level.

## 5. Regression and disposition

The rejected `Q`-only charge is not used.  A pentagonal exceptional face may have
five off-phase side occurrences while contributing only one unit to `Q`; this is
consistent with the present `U`-based statement.  Bridges are counted by physical
edge in the side charge and by occurrences only where explicitly stated; no silent
identification is made.

The candidate narrows the live equality corridor to a linear unpaid off-phase
population.  It supplies neither an upper bound on that population nor a strict
diameter descent, exact unit triangle, or Level-I conclusion.

**Final verdict:** PASS as audited-working/proof-only research.  C-II remains open.
